        {
          "id": "note",
          "kind": "note",
          "data": {
            "text": "## Array lab\n\nThree ways of measuring an array rather than listening to one. All three read the same `array` wire, so they report at once, but they do not want the same antennas: read each one's note before trusting its numbers.\n\n**Spatial spectrum** is bearing against frequency: a waterfall whose vertical axis is direction. Use it to see which direction each signal in the band arrives from, before pointing anything. It wants the elements spread apart, as the ring here has them.\n\n**Correlator** reports the raw baseline visibilities, the per-pair phase and amplitude the other nodes consume as a middle step. When a direction finder gives nonsense, this is where you look: bad visibilities mean a cabling or calibration fault, not a bad algorithm.\n\n**Polarimeter** is the odd one out. It wants two crossed antennas at one point, not a ring, so to use it properly rebuild the Array geometry for your actual pair and set `h_lane` and `v_lane` to them. It reports the wave's polarisation, and its beam output is the combination matched to that, which recovers a signal a single fixed antenna hears badly. That output is wired to the **Channel** and **Speaker**, so you can hear the difference the matching makes.\n\nThe ring is 0.35 m, the app's default. Set it to your real aperture and calibrate before trusting any of them."
          },
